{"step_index":125,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T06:57:48Z","input_tokens":209,"cache_read_tokens":220389,"output_tokens":3589,"thinking":"The discovery of the `ShaderNodeShaderToRGB` node within Blender 5.3 EEVEE presents a direct solution. This node natively facilitates the conversion needed, eliminating the prior concerns about external tools or workarounds. This node, coupled with the proper backface culling settings, addresses the core requirement of converting shaders for the necessary effect.\n\n\nThe native backface culling in Blender 5.3 EEVEE provides a performant shadow solution, particularly beneficial for ink outline effects without the need for raycasting. The built-in CompositorNodeKuwahara filter enables achieving *Across the Spider-Verse* style painterly effects on specific elements, using depth masks for control. Further exploration of Compositor nodes like Filter, Posterize, and color separation, alongside depth-driven offsets, unlocks additional artistic possibilities. The availability of full EEVEE render passes offers detailed control for compositing.\n\n\nThe target machine is a Pure CPU EEVEE system, making raytracing prohibitively slow. Disabling raytracing in favor of the `ShaderToRGB`, `Kuwahara`, and normal/Z ink lines is expected to render significantly faster, potentially halving the rendering time. Reviewing the project `README.md` reveals a critical directory hygiene rule for project file organization. The rule restricts the `LATEST/` directory to only three specific blend files.\n\n\nThe current task involves creating a distinct edition, necessitating a strict separation from the existing V5 setup. The new Spider-Verse edition, designated V6, will operate as an independent entity. This separation ensures the integrity of the original V5 project while allowing for a clean implementation of stylized effects, utilizing V5 assets as read-only inputs. The structure will isolate all V6 components, including scripts, renders, and assets, to prevent unintended modifications to the V5 pipeline.\n\n\nHalftones use **crosshatching** and **stepped shading**, achieving a non-photorealistic (NPR) material system. Instead of flat cartoon shading, highlights are broken up with screen-space Ben-Day halftone dots, while midtones use crosshatching and core shadows are rendered with dense, irregular crosshatches. Depth cues are reinforced by the crosshatch density, avoiding a flat look. This shading system adds detail and depth.\n\n\nShadows utilize diagonal parallel tonal hatching, or ink lines, using rotated procedural waves and lines. Density in the shadow controls line thickness. Rim lighting employs stepped electric cyan, hot magenta, and solar amber Fresnel rim kicks. These wrap around aero curves, bargeboards, the Halo, and wings. Stepped reflections on the bodywork include sharp graphic highlights on the nosecone and sidepods.\n\nThe 3D hero car uses a Solidify modifier to create the inverted-hull silhouette and feature crease ink. This includes an offset of one, flipped normals, and backface culling. This modifier utilizes a separate material index with backface culling enabled in shadow.\n\n\nInitial ink lines utilize procedural noise and vertex modulation for varying line thickness, simulating brush pen strokes. Compositor node filters, coupled with depth passes, create sharp interior ink lines on various scene elements, with depth-based scaling to prevent distant details from becoming overwhelming. The challenge is separating painterly environment backgrounds from the hero's sharp ink art. Using Cryptomatte and Mist passes is being explored to maintain this crucial stylistic distinction.\n\n\nThe plan is to apply a painterly effect to the mid/far environment and sky using Blender 5.3's Kuwahara filter combined with Posterize and color shifts, creating an expressive, artistic background. This will make the sharply rendered hero car stand out, drawing focus. The goal for out-of-focus areas is to use depth-driven chromatic misregistration, not Gaussian blur, splitting the color channels based on distance from the focal plane.\n\n\nThe telephoto pans require a specific comic-book print aesthetic, achieved by rendering ink lines with an offset along diagonal print angles. This offset will create the desired visual effect across various telephoto shots. Next, I will shift focus to applying this effect to the `Action Speed-Ribbons` for a more complete result.\n\n\nFocusing on frame rate and motion style, the goal is to replicate the *Across the Spider-Verse* visual rhythm, balancing smooth camera movements with stepped animation for secondary elements like the car and speed effects. A hybrid approach will be implemented where camera motion remains on ones at 24fps for smoothness, while car motion and graphic overlays step on twos at 12fps. Smooth motion blur should be disabled or significantly reduced to embrace the show's stylistic crispness and substitute it with graphical streak effects.\n\n\nThe *Across the Spider-Verse* reference suggests a structured visual rhythm across the 90-second lap. This rhythm can be split into three distinct visual dimensions for Silverstone's three sectors, each associated with a different Spider-Verse Earth: Brooklyn Classic Comic, Gwen Stacy Expressionist Watercolor, and Nueva York 2099 Cyberpunk Graphic. This division allows the incorporation of a variety of techniques within a single video. This approach provides a palette and artistic direction for applying stylized effects.\n\n\nThe goal is to integrate the distinct visual styles of *Across the Spider-Verse* across the Silverstone track sectors and implement a style selection feature for flexible visual control. Defining a configurable palette system via command-line options allows instant switching between visual styles.\n\nAn *audio remix* will blend the telemetry audio with specific *Spider-Verse* sonic signatures. The plan artifact (`spiderverse_v6_plan.md`) must adhere to strict artifact creation guidelines, especially those in `<artifacts>`. The plan will be saved with appropriate metadata for feedback and user accessibility, in a dedicated directory.\n\n\nThe project plan needs a `Details` section, a visual *Architecture* diagram, and a Swarm execution phase breakdown.  It should include Github-style alerts to emphasize key points, following the *<artifacts>* creation rule and `<communication_style>` guidelines.  I will focus on including clickable `file://` links to facilitate quick access to referenced files. The plan will also incorporate clear technical pipeline design and a performance budget.\n\n\n"}
