{"step_index":139,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T07:13:15Z","input_tokens":2883,"cache_read_tokens":230096,"output_tokens":9636,"thinking":"Initial observation: several key components are in a \"waiting\" state, already possessing the complete V5 scene structure. This suggests a potential bottleneck or dependency on an external trigger. Further investigation is needed to determine the exact nature of this wait.\n\n\nV6 file management demands strict isolation from V5 to prevent corruption. A read-only lock will be enforced on all V5 files, preventing edits and overwrites. New V6 files will be managed in a dedicated `v6_spiderverse` directory with clear ownership assigned to specific components. The overall strategy involves creating a V6 swarm title, and assigning specific tasks to the relevant components.\n\n\nThe `textures.py` script populates the `v6_spiderverse/textures/` directory with stylized Spider-Verse textures. The `pipeline/v6_sv_build.py` script reads the V5 animation file in a read-only mode to prevent corruption, outputting a V6 lookdev blend file and preview renders. The build script configures Eevee for NPR rendering, optimizing the scene for a stylized comic aesthetic.\n\n\nThe target graphic style employs tailored Spider-Verse palettes and surface shaders across the car and track. The car's livery, including matte navy, forged carbon, and tires, will be transformed into comic-style armor, crosshatched slate, and inked rubber with graphic elements. The track environment gets matte asphalt, bold kerbs, stylized grass, comic hoardings, and expressive sky dome with dimension-based color keyframing.\n\nThe plan involves adding several 3D graphic effects. These include variable-width hero ink outlines using an inverted-hull solidify modifier with emission, backface culling, and vertex weight modulation. Also, 3D comic aero vortex ribbons will be added to the rear wing and floor, and wheel-spin comic smear arcs will be added to the wheels. Build time and preview metrics will be verified.\n\n\nThe `@fx` component details the visual effects pipeline, documenting shader inputs and object naming conventions. This phase hands off the scene to `@anim`. The `@anim` component handles the 3-act color script and animation, using Blender 5.3's compositor for Spider-Verse style effects, producing stills, a comic page poster, and motion clips.\n\n\nThe plan involves generating three 4-second motion clips, each representing a different Spider-Verse aesthetic, rendered at 12fps: the Earth-1610 Miles comic style, Earth-65 Gwen watercolor, and Earth-928 2099 cyber-neon. The clips will be rendered to frames and named according to a specified naming convention. The relevant documentation and the README file will be updated to reflect the new V6 section. The compositing script implements safe animation stepping at 12fps, preserving crucial world and camera data.\n\n\nTransformations will be smoothed to 24 fps to prevent chase-cam strobing, but car-local secondary dynamics will be stepped to 12 fps with constant keying to match the comic aesthetic. The motion blur shutter is reduced to 0.08 to ensure crisp frames.\n\nA 3-act dimensional color script will be implemented with keyframes for `SV_Dimension` transitioning between universes, with chromatic glitch spikes at transition cuts and stark black/white/crimson impact frames at key moments. The compositor will be enabled.\n\n\nThe plan involves setting up render passes, including normal, mist, Z, emission, and cryptomatte data for compositing. A three-stage compositing approach will generate the stylized effects. Stage 1 applies an anisotropic filter to the background based on mist, leaving the car crisp. Stage 2 uses Sobel/Kirsch filters on normal and mist passes to add ink-like lines to creases based on depth. Stage 3 creates a CMYK/misregistration effect based on depth relative to camera focus.\n\n\nStage 4 applies procedural paper grain, speed-line vignettes based on velocity exceeding a threshold, and 2-frame duotone impact frames. The compositing pipeline then renders stills, a comic page, and three showcase clips. Finally, the process hands off to sound effects.\n\nThe sound effects component will create a Spider-Verse sonic signature using telemetry-synced engine sounds and a 108 BPM track. This will incorporate comic upshift sounds, 808 sub-bass drops on impact frames, and dimensional glitch effects tied to the color script.\n\n\nUniverse shift transitions require unique sonic cues: a brass-synth distort for the Prowler-esque portal sound at the specified frame numbers. Three distinct sonic palettes will accompany each act, changing from a punchy comic sound to a lush, dreamy chorus, and finally to a cyber-saturated synth growl.\n\nA mux script will combine the showcase clips with precisely time-sliced audio, creating a 12-second reel, enabling immediate playback. This script also supports muxing the full animation with the final audio mix when renders are complete, with the sound design components running in parallel.\n\n\nRender passes and performance metrics are essential, especially within the composite. The verifier will validate the complete rendering. The pipeline should generate 3 clips, then combine those with the 12-second showcase clip. This begins as soon as the animation is finished.\n\n\nWorld and camera tracking smoothing to 24 fps is being implemented while local stepping occurs, and initial CPU render speed is being measured at approximately 1.5–3.0 seconds per frame. The verifier will quantitatively assess all rendered stills and frame sequences, checking for correct color palettes and registration. Further checks involve verifying the audio sync, quality, and resolution of all clips and documentation.\n\n\nCarefully verifying the clip's frame ranges to ensure synchronization between animation and sound components is critical. Frame-accurate timestamps are being calculated to ensure alignment, especially given that sound design relies on precise timing. Specifically, the clips have been analyzed, and timestamps, and frame numbers were generated.\n\n\nDetermining the precise frame ranges for each clip is crucial for synchronizing the animation and sound effects, especially given the precise timing needed for the sound design. Carefully analyzing the animation for key visual moments is essential for accurate frame range generation, allowing for sonic and visual synchronization. The selection of clips is confirmed: Earth-1610 Miles, Earth-65 Gwen, and Earth-928 2099 Cyber Neon.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"title","title":"blender-supercar V6 蜘蛛侠：纵横宇宙风格化版本"}},{"name":"post_message","args":{"text":"@monitor resume — New phase started: V6 Spider-Verse Stylized Edition (`@env-2` -> `@anim` -> `@sfx` -> `@verifier` -> `@coordinator`)."}},{"name":"post_message","args":{"text":"@env-2 **NEW PHASE — V6 SPIDER-VERSE STYLIZED EDITION (LOOKDEV, 3D INK & ACTION FX)**\n\nSTRICT RULES:\n1) **100% V5 Isolation**: NEVER modify any V5 file (`LATEST/v5_*`, `pipeline/v5_*`, `renders_v5/*`, `textures/*`, `docs/README_V5.md`). Read `LATEST/v5_stage_anim.blend` as read-only upstream input.\n2) **No Binary `read_file` (#7)**: NEVER call `read_file` on `.jpg`/`.png`/`.mp4`/`.wav`/`.blend`. Use Python PIL/NumPy scripts for all visual checks.\n3) **You OWN**: `pipeline/v6_sv_textures.py`, `v6_spiderverse/textures/*`, `pipeline/v6_sv_build.py`, `v6_spiderverse/v6_stage_lookdev.blend`, and `renders_v6/preview/*`.\n\nYOUR DELIVERABLES:\n1) `pipeline/v6_sv_textures.py` -> generates stylized Spider-Verse textures in `v6_spiderverse/textures/` (`sv_pit_garage.png`, `sv_ad_boards.png`, `sv_bigscreen.png`, `sv_brake_150/100/50.png`, `sv_drs_board.png`, `sv_paper_grain.png`): bold hand-inked borders, Ben-Day halftone shading, comic diagonal slashes, vibrant Spider-Verse color blocks (no copyrighted Marvel logos; keep F1 racing theme!).\n2) 